# Argon OS — XFCE desktop variant
# Recommends are globally disabled (--apt-recommends false), so everything
# the desktop needs must be listed explicitly.

## Display server & session
xorg
xserver-xorg-input-libinput
xserver-xorg-video-all
# xrandr — used by Argon's login-time wallpaper script to target the real
# connected monitor outputs (recommends are off, so list it explicitly).
x11-xserver-utils
lightdm
lightdm-gtk-greeter
light-locker
# dbus-user-session, NOT dbus-x11. This is the actual reason audio was
# dead: PipeWire/WirePlumber run under the systemd --user manager and
# reach each other over the per-user session bus at $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/bus,
# which logind+pam_systemd only set up when dbus-user-session is
# installed. With the legacy dbus-x11 instead, wireplumber.service dies
# with "Failed to connect to session bus" and no audio nodes are ever
# created — Debian bug #998167 / #1032351, and exactly our symptom. Their
# enable symlinks were present all along (the packages ship them); the bus
# they connect to was not. Debian's own XFCE desktop task ships
# dbus-user-session; recommends being off is why we didn't get it.
dbus-user-session
polkitd
pkexec
mate-polkit

## XFCE core
xfce4
xfce4-terminal
xfce4-power-manager
xfce4-notifyd
xfce4-screenshooter
xfce4-taskmanager
xfce4-whiskermenu-plugin
xfce4-pulseaudio-plugin
thunar
thunar-archive-plugin
mousepad
ristretto
xarchiver

## Theming (dark, minimal, Nordic)
arc-theme
papirus-icon-theme
fonts-noto-core
fonts-noto-mono
fonts-firacode
librsvg2-bin

## Applications
firefox-esr
network-manager-gnome
pavucontrol
pipewire
pipewire-pulse
wireplumber
# The pieces of a working audio stack that recommends-off would otherwise
# drop. Native PipeWire/Pulse apps (Firefox, etc.) play through pipewire +
# wireplumber alone, but these complete it:
#   pipewire-alsa          — routes plain ALSA apps through PipeWire
#   libspa-0.2-bluetooth   — Bluetooth audio (A2DP/HFP); pairs with the
#                            bluez/blueman stack, without it a paired
#                            headset connects but plays nothing
#   rtkit                  — realtime scheduling so audio doesn't crackle
#                            under load (wireplumber asks rtkit for it)
pipewire-alsa
libspa-0.2-bluetooth
rtkit
gvfs
gvfs-backends
xdg-user-dirs
xdg-utils

## fail2ban systemd backend (recommends are off)
python3-systemd

## Runtime for Argon's own apps (argon-apps.deb via packages.chroot)
python3-gi
gir1.2-gtk-3.0

## Media & codecs — play common audio/video out of the box + thumbnails
ffmpeg
gstreamer1.0-plugins-good
gstreamer1.0-plugins-bad
gstreamer1.0-plugins-ugly
gstreamer1.0-libav
# (hardware VA-API accel: add gstreamer1.0-va once confirmed in the archive;
#  gstreamer1.0-vaapi was dropped from Debian/Kali. Software decode via the
#  plugins above already plays everything.)
tumbler
tumbler-plugins-extra
ffmpegthumbnailer
parole
fonts-noto-color-emoji
fonts-liberation2
fonts-dejavu

## Desktop polish — clipboard manager, night-light filter
xfce4-clipman-plugin
redshift-gtk

## Bluetooth — the stack was missing entirely; headsets/mice are table
## stakes on laptops. blueman is the tray/manager UI, bluez the daemon.
## The service is enabled by the services hook; it does not listen on the
## network.
bluez
blueman

## Optional Wayland session (experimental). XFCE X11 stays the default; this
## adds a selectable "Argon (Wayland)" session at the login screen for those
## who want it — labwc (a small wlroots compositor) with a waybar panel,
## swaybg wallpaper and a fuzzel launcher. Terminal/apps run under XWayland.
labwc
waybar
swaybg
fuzzel

## A little flash — fast, pretty system-info in the terminal
fastfetch

## Pulsemeeter (GUI Voicemeeter alternative) installs per-user via pipx on
## first launch — these are its runtime prerequisites. pipx does the
## isolated install; pulseaudio-utils gives it pactl to drive PipeWire's
## Pulse layer; python3-venv is what pipx builds the environment with.
pipx
python3-venv
pulseaudio-utils

## Faster & lighter — compressed RAM swap + OOM protection on low-memory boxes
zram-tools
earlyoom

## More default apps — calculator, disk usage, PDF viewer
galculator
baobab
atril

## Flatpak — optional sandboxed apps from Flathub, surfaced in Argon
## Software. Nothing is installed by default; a first-boot service just adds
## the Flathub remote, and Argon Software installs flatpaks per-user (no
## root). xdg-desktop-portal-gtk lets sandboxed apps open files/links.
flatpak
xdg-desktop-portal
xdg-desktop-portal-gtk

## Installer (live session only)
calamares
# unsquashfs — Calamares' unpackfs step unpacks the live filesystem.squashfs
# onto the target with this. Without it the install fails immediately with
# "Failed to find unsquashfs".
squashfs-tools
# Boot loader for the INSTALLED system. Calamares' grubcfg/bootloader steps
# run grub-install + update-grub in the target, so these must be present in
# the image. The -bin variants (not the grub-pc/grub-efi metapackages) are
# used deliberately: they carry the platform modules without the interactive
# "install to which disk?" debconf prompt that would stall the chroot build.
grub-common
grub2-common
grub-pc-bin
grub-efi-amd64-bin
efibootmgr
os-prober
